+## What this run was
+
+Seed `fabro-22e4` (tar-upload mtime = 1970 bug), one full develop cycle with **one gate-red bounce**: planner 43.8s/$0.061 → implementer@1 411s/$0.135 → tester RED (11s) → gatebounce → implementer@2 464s/$0.405 → tester GREEN (41s) → evidence → reviewer 35.7s/$0.040 → closeout. Total ~17.5 min active, **$0.642, of which the implementer stages were $0.54 (84%)** (all figures from the run's conclusion/billing in run events).
+
+## Recommendations, ordered by expected impact
+
+**1. Make the implementer's mandated pre-gate clippy mirror the gate's feature set.**
+*What happened:* The prompt (`.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/implementer.md`, step 4) mandates `cargo clippy -p <touched-crate> --all-targets -- -D warnings` — default features, exactly what the gate runs. The implementer instead verified only under `--features docker` (its summary: "compile check via `cargo nextest run -p fabro-sandbox --features docker`… clippy shows only two pre-existing errors in provider.rs/sandbox_spec.rs" — docker-only lints). The default-features E0432 (`managed_labels`) it had *already diagnosed in its own journal painpoint* then red-lined the tester 11 seconds in.
+*Change:* In `implementer.md` step 4, add: "Run the literal gate invocation (`cargo clippy -p <crate> --all-targets -- -D warnings`, default features) on every touched crate, in addition to any feature-scoped checks for feature-gated code; a known default-features break in a touched crate makes criterion 'gate passes' FAIL, not PASS-under-features."
+*Expected effect:* This exact class of bounce (green self-check, red gate) is caught pre-submit. Here it would have saved the entire second cycle — ~8 min wall and ~$0.41, i.e. 64% of run cost.
+
+**2. Permit (and require disclosing) minimal repair of pre-existing compile breaks in touched crates.**
+*What happened:* implementer@1 verified the E0432 was pre-existing (stash-reproduce on planner commit `1e3f7fc`), knew the exact fix ("move the cfg gate onto the pub use"), but "nothing more, nothing less" scope plus report-don't-fix pushed it to a painpoint instead of a 3-line fix — and the gate punished the whole run for it.
+*Change:* Same file, `implementer.md`: add a carve-out — "A verified pre-existing compile/clippy break **in a touched crate** may be fixed minimally to keep the gate green; name it in `implementation_summary` as adjacent repair."
+*Expected effect:* Gate-blocking latent rot gets fixed in-pass instead of burning a bounce cycle; reviewers already accept this pattern (reviewer journal: "compile-repair… not scope creep").
+
+**3. Stop dropping the implementer's own prior diagnosis on gate-red bounces.**
+*What happened:* implementer@2's prompt (visible in stage events) carried the tester failure tail and gatebounce hits, but **not** `implementation_summary` or `journal` — the node's `preamble_allow_keys` deliberately excludes them ("the implementer's OWN outputs echoing back"). So the bounce pass re-derived from scratch what implementer@1 had root-caused 90 seconds earlier, at 305s inference / 6,997 reasoning tokens / $0.405 — 73% of the run's entire reasoning spend.
+*Change:* In `workflow.fabro`, implementer node: add `implementation_summary` to `preamble_allow_keys` (or have the bounce path inject the previous pass's painpoints as a `gate_bounce_notes` key).
+*Expected effect:* Bounce passes start at the known root cause instead of re-diagnosing; on this run that's roughly half of implementer@2's inference.
+
+**4. Normalize stale mtimes deterministically until the transport fix ships.**
+*What happened:* The seed's own bug sabotaged its own fix — implementer@2's journal: after `edit_file` writes, `push_credentials.rs` carried mtime 1970-01-01, "cargo check replayed stale dead-code warnings… line numbers that no longer matched the source," forcing a manual `touch` detour (lesson `mx-c28106`; same class previously produced wrong test failures in run `01M1YBBXR99PNME6Y8ZED4Y2NN`).
+*Change:* `scripts/qualitygate.nu`: at start, `find lib -name '*.rs' ! -newermt 2000-01-01 -exec touch {} +` (and mirror the one-liner in `implementer.md` step 4) until the rebuilt sandbox binary is everywhere.
+*Expected effect:* Zero-LLM-cost elimination of stale-fingerprint false diagnoses — the failure mode this seed exists to fix, still live in every run container.
+
+**5. Give the evidence capture an inline floor in the preamble demote pass.**
+*What happened:* The 8.6 KB capture was blob-ref'd to the reviewer despite `preamble_inline_max_kb=16` — because the 24 KB aggregate budget was consumed by **duplicate** content: the green tester output rendered twice, plus an irrelevant 1.4 KB gatebounce hit (reviewer's own painpoint: "had to be paged manually before judging"). Open seeds `fabro-b568`/`fabro-8d2c`/`fabro-cf3e` already point here.
+*Change:* Engine preamble demote pass (or graph `preamble_budget_kb` 24→32 plus dedupe per `fabro-edac`): `command.output` on the reviewer node is demoted last / never while it fits `preamble_inline_max_kb`.
+*Expected effect:* Small captures review inline — one fewer tool round-trip per review and removal of the unread-blob → "Verification blocked" failure mode.
+
+**6. Tighten gate-bounce matching to error signatures.**
+*What happened:* The E0432/cargo failure matched known-bug seed `fabro-4814` ("purge retired two-worlds framing" — a prompt-refactoring task, utterly unrelated), injecting ~1.4 KB of noise into both implementer@2's and the reviewer's preambles.
+*Change:* `.fabro/workflows/develop/scripts/gate-bounce.nu`: require token overlap with the failure's error code/crate/file path, not label-level matching; emit `{"hits":[]}` when nothing real matches.
+*Expected effect:* Bounce context carries only signal; less misdirection for the next implementer, budget back for the evidence that matters.
+
+**7. Fix the "Pipeline progress: X of 7" denominator.**
+*What happened:* implementer@1's prompt read "Pipeline progress: 0 of 7 stages completed" *after* the planner completed; the terminal view says "9 of 7" (seeds `fabro-45bf`/`fabro-a0e3`/`38f4`).
+*Change:* Engine stage-prompt builder: count unique completed nodes, not stage-section count.
+*Expected effect:* Stage prompts stop contradicting reality — cosmetic, but it's the first line every agent reads.
+
+The common thread: this run's cost and duration were dominated not by the seed work (implementer@1 was clean and cheap) but by **one avoidable gate-red bounce** — recs 1–3 attack it from the check side, the scope side, and the context side; 4–7 remove the residual friction the journal documented in the run's own words.
